      "review": "Review and narrowly correct the existing efficient inference survey using primary sources. Preserve source URL/provenance records and valid content; all edits/finalizer/validator inside sandbox, no program edits.\n\n1. FlashAttention https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135 is exact IO-aware attention: reduces HBM reads/writes and memory footprint, but does not remove the quadratic arithmetic complexity of dense self-attention. Correct any TL;DR claim that it reduces quadratic computation complexity.\n2. PagedAttention https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180 enables near-zero KV-cache waste, avoiding external fragmentation and bounding last-block/internal waste. Do not claim all internal/external fragmentation is completely eliminated or that it reduces attention's quadratic arithmetic complexity.\n3. Distinguish training results from inference results. FlashAttention abstract benchmark speedups concern training; do not present them as universal inference gains. Qualify throughput numbers by hardware, workload, baseline and evaluation setting.\n4. TinyLlama https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.02385 is a 1.1B pretrained model, not distilled BERT. DistilBERT is an encoder understanding model, not an autoregressive generation model. Preserve this distinction and avoid unsupported edge-throughput claims.\n5. Replace any raw bracketed paper identifier such as [2501.09223] with the correct numeric citation if that source is listed, or remove the unsupported claim. Check every substantial sentence and every [n] against its actual source.\n6. Use exact heading ## Trends and open problems. Differentiate algorithmic speculative decoding's exact target distribution from approximate sparse/quantized methods. Do not generalize isolated benchmark speedups to all systems.\n\nFetch these primary pages to verify precise corrections; do not add new sources or relabel existing ones.\n",
      "before_report_sha256": "b3de4032c27d2bb96dccc04845a26ae60cc1689d7dd370c7f5b5de816e7a0aa0"
    },
